{"name":"Greens","hex":"#016844","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Greens","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of Green"]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["plural of Green","The Australian Greens, a left-wing, progressive green party in Australia"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Greens"}],"entries":[{"title":"Leaf vegetable","blocks":[{"text":"Leaf vegetables, also called leafy greens, vegetable greens, or simply greens, are plant leaves eaten as a vegetable. Those eaten raw in a salad can be called salad greens, whereas leaf vegetables eaten cooked can be called pot herbs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Spinach_leaves.jpg/330px-Spinach_leaves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf_vegetable","wikidata":"Q20134"},{"title":"Green politics","blocks":[{"text":"Green politics, or ecopolitics, is a political ideology that aims to foster an ecologically sustainable society often, but not always, rooted in environmentalism, nonviolence, social justice and grassroots democracy. It began taking shape in the Western world in the 1970s; since then, green parties have developed and established themselves in many countries around the globe and have achieved some electoral success."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Sunflower10094.jpg/330px-Sunflower10094.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_politics","wikidata":"Q1661415"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Kale-Bundle.jpg/500px-Kale-Bundle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Kale-Bundle.jpg/1280px-Kale-Bundle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thu